==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|attercoppe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ātorcoppe|t=spider}}, corresponding to {{compound|en|atter|gloss1=poison, venom|cop|gloss2=spider}}. The latter is still to be found in the English word {{m|en|cobweb}}. Cognate to {{cog|nl|etterkop||peevish or ill-natured person}} and {{cog|da|edderkop||spider}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈætəkɒp/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|except in dialects}} A [[spider]].
#* '''1924''', {{w|Robert Graves}}, “Attercop: the All-Wise Spider”:
#*: Myself, not bound by James’ view<br>Nor Walter’s, in a vision saw these two<br>Like trapped and weakening flies<br>In toils of the same hoary net;<br>I seemed to hear ancestral cries<br>Buzzing ‘To our All-Wise, Omnivorous<br>'''Attercop''' glowering over us,<br>Whose table we have set<br>With blood and bones and sweat.’
#* '''1937''', {{w|J. R. R. Tolkien}}, ''{{w|The Hobbit}}'':
#*: Old fat spider spinning in a tree!<br>Old fat spider can’t see me!<br>'''Attercop'''! '''Attercop'''!<br>Won’t you stop,<br>Stop your spinning and look for me?
# {{lb|en|obsolete|except in dialects}} A [[peevish]] or [[ill-natured]] person.

